  "raw_meta_yaml": "schema_version: 1\n\ncontext:\n  name: rea-agents\n  version: \"6.10.1\"\n\npackage:\n  name: ${{ name }}\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  url: https://registry.npmjs.org/${{ name }}/-/${{ name }}-${{ version }}.tgz\n  sha256: e8cf08c9fa954e998fc8868c23fa8e5f518cdb9553488ec227b31a3bb6903150\n\nbuild:\n  number: 0\n  # The tarball bundles only a win32-x64 native addon\n  skip:\n    - target_platform == \"win-arm64\"\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    - nodejs\n  host:\n    - nodejs\n  run:\n    - nodejs >=22.23.4,<23.0a0 | >=24.22.1,<25.0a0 | >=26.12.1\n    # README: static JS/Electron analysis on Linux needs a procps-compatible `ps`\n    - if: linux\n      then: procps-ng\n\ntests:\n  - script:\n      - rea --help\n      - rea-agents --help\n\nabout:\n  homepage: https://rea.tools\n  repository: https://github.com/morluto/rea\n  summary: Reverse engineer anything from your terminal or agent with one CLI and MCP server.\n  description: |\n    REA connects agents (via MCP) and the terminal to tools for inspecting\n    native binaries, JavaScript/Electron apps, .NET assemblies, Android APKs\n    and websites. Native analysis uses an existing Hopper, Ghidra or IDA\n    installation, which is not installed by this package.\n  license: MIT\n  license_file:\n    - LICENSE\n    - THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md\n    - licenses/\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - jsmolic\n",
